ients with height the highest  concentrations of pro neuro Tencent and  circulation and in plasma are at the  highest risk of incident breast cancer  and it's important that that you realize  this is incident breast cancer and not  recurrence which really is is great to  have a new biomarker in this area for  potentially identifying patients for  incidence of breast cancer so for this  reason we in the laboratory as well as  our clinical colleagues found this to be  a promising biomarker and we're not as  far along with evaluating this one but  we're well on our way so we've actually  already completed our analytical  validation very similar steps that that  I outlined for the for the studies that  we perform with pro and kefflin so I  won't go through them again here but  because the assay platform is very  similar it's another Eliza based assay  very similar analytic valid evaluation  that we did and all parameters performed  really very nicely throughout the  clinically relevant measuring range and  so on the next steps that we have  ongoing are that we're evaluating a  clinical evaluation study on partnering  the laboratory with our clinical  colleagues and collecting patient  samples to be able to evaluate this  biomarker and our own patient population  here at Mayo Clinic  through the collaboration with mango  tech we've actually formed a formal  collaborative research partnership such  that we at Mayo Clinic will be  performing these research studies with  Mayo Clinic patient samples but then  we've also opened this project up to be  a testing site for outside researchers  who have interests in the biomarkers  that are produced by Sango Tech the two  biomarkers that I presented here both  Pro and Catalan and Pro neuro Tenten as  well as other biomarkers under  development at Bingo tech such that we  would be a testing site for researchers  outside of Mayo Clinic who actually  might want to do some research on these  by using these biomarkers in their own  patient samples but don't have the means  to bring up the assay themselves and  this is a unique partnership between  Stingo tech and Mayo Clinic that offers  us a unique research perspective here at  Mayo Clinic to further patient care for  the research and hopefully improve the  health of our patients and those  everywhere around the world  so in conclusion today I hope what we  have done is given you a sense of what  it might take to transition a biomart  from the basic research setting again a  setting where either basic researchers  or companies in either the academic  field or in the you know more private  private area have actually done the  research to develop these assays  identifying the biomarkers of interest  and develop the even develop assays but  haven't transitioned it to clinical use  so I'm hoping you a sense of the efforts  that it takes in the clinical laboratory  to take that research and do the end of  both the analytical and the clinical  evaluations in a hospital setting to  then be be confident in the assay  performance both analytically that  you're getting the right number and  clinically that the the number that you  get will be useful for patient care it  certainly is a lot of work is time  consuming but hopefully with a lot of  gains for the for patient care at the  end  so with that we would certainly like to  end and take any any questions  absolutely thank you dr. Jaffe dr.  Newsome and dr. Donato for your  presentations a quick reminder for our  audience on how to submit questions  simply type them into the drop-down box  located on the far left of your  presentation window labeled ask a  question and click on the send button  the speakers will answer as many  questions as time permits so let's see  our first question of the day doctors  asks how do you learn about new  biomarkers and partner with basic  researchers or companies that have  discovered them  you  so thank you for the question we we're  at a large academic Medical Center 